  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 30004078,
   "parent_id": 20132297,
   "parent_type": "question",
   "author_user_id": 33213,
   "author_display_name": "Stack Overflow is garbage",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/20132297/#comment30004078_20132297",
   "score": 1,
   "created_utc": "2013-11-21T21:41:39Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "Your logic is faulty. When a tool reports that there is a memory leak in your code then that does not necessarily mean that there is a memory leak in your code. It could also be a shortcoming or bug in the leak detection tool -- or incorrect usage of said tool. In this case, your <code>unique_ptr</code> will <i>not</i> leak memory."
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 20132771,
   "parent_id": 20132297,
   "author_user_id": 214796,
   "author_display_name": "Collin Dauphinee",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": true,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/20132771",
   "score": 8,
   "created_utc": "2013-11-21T21:55:00Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2013-11-21T21:55:00Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>This happens if you attempt to check for leaks before static destruction has occurred. </p>\n\n<p>To fix this, you can call <a href=\"http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/5at7yxcs%28v=vs.71%29.aspx\" rel=\"noreferrer\">_CrtSetDbgFlag</a> with <code>_CRTDBG_LEAK_CHECK_DF</code> at the beginning of your application; it will automatically invoke <code>_CrtDumpMemoryLeaks</code> at exit, after static destruction.</p>\n"
  },
  {
